# Ignore rules for deploy/trc/Dockerfile ONLY.
#
# BuildKit prefers `<dockerfile-path>.dockerignore` over the context-root
# `.dockerignore` when it exists, and does NOT merge the two -- so this file is
# a full copy of the root one (upstream's rules, above the TRC block at the
# bottom) rather than an overlay. Keeping it here is what lets the root
# `.dockerignore` stay byte-identical to upstream.
#
# If an older BuildKit ignores this file and falls back to the root rules, the
# cost is transfer time only, not correctness: the frontend stage enumerates
# its COPY sources, so nothing extra in the context can invalidate a layer.

.github
.DS_Store
docs
kubernetes
node_modules
/.svelte-kit
/package
.env
.env.*
vite.config.js.timestamp-*
vite.config.ts.timestamp-*
__pycache__
.idea
venv
_old
uploads
.ipynb_checkpoints
**/*.db
_test
backend/data/*

# TRC: keep the build context small and, more importantly, STABLE.
#
# .git is ~400 MB in this repo and its contents change on EVERY commit. Leaving
# it in the context meant that context was tarred and streamed over SSH to the
# deploy host twice per deploy (the frontend-only prebuild and the full build),
# and that the frontend stage's COPY was invalidated by every commit whether or
# not a single frontend file moved -- so `npm run build` never once hit the
# layer cache the whole build-on-the-deploy-host arrangement exists to exploit.
#
# The trade is deliberate and already precedented: svelte.config.js falls back
# from `git rev-parse HEAD` to the package.json version when git is absent, so
# SvelteKit's version string no longer changes per commit (it drives the
# poll-and-offer-reload mechanic, nothing else). The deploy workflow already
# refuses to pass BUILD_HASH as a build-arg for exactly this reason -- it
# records the commit as an image LABEL instead. Keeping .git in the context
# quietly defeated that decision.
.git
.venv
.ruff_cache
/build
